 is kind of a dirty little  secret about  educators there's like the you know the  brilliant students  who are going to do great no matter what  and you might foster them  there's the students who are going to  struggle and sometimes  it's because they're they're out of  their  their league where they are sometimes  it's because  they have bad preparation beforehand  it's a product of previous education  sometimes it's due to character issues  like not wanting to work  but for whatever reason there are  students who struggle right  and then there's the vast majority of  students who are you know more or less  on track but  they do require some some attention some  structure  and uh who do you teach to  i mean if your goal is to produce  geniuses you can say well  forget these other kids i'm going to  focus on these kids  because they're the ones who are  probably going to be the greatest  benefit to the community later on down  the line  and if each one of them let's have a  class of 20.  each one of them is going to benefit  thousands of people and improve their  lives  maybe it's not so bad if i if i let the  five bad students fall through the  cracks  let's say there's five really good  students and five really bad students  and then everybody in the middle  and the other kids in the middle they're  not going to get as good of an education  and they're not going to be as happy but  it's okay because i'm gonna benefit  thousands  what do you think from utilitarian  perspective is that  is that a good  way to allocate my my resources  yeah how do i know they're going to  benefit thousands this is where that  that  issue of probability comes in  there's lots of bright people  you guys know lots of bright people are  they all productive  do they all benefit society some of them  turn to evil  don't they don't they use their talents  for that  did you ever hand them out okay um  you know if the odds aren't that good  then maybe actually i should be  investing my  my time and effort into the the kids  that i actually have in the classroom  imagine those k-12 right um  maybe i should actually be spending my  resources on the struggling students  and giving very little attention to the  super bright students because they're  going to take care of themselves and the  bulk of my attention to  the students in the middle and then to  the students who are really struggling  maybe that would have the better payoff  wouldn't it  maybe we should have greater resources  for that  if you only could have remedial programs  or a gifted and talented program  you have to cut one of them which should  be key  it's a big question isn't it these are  the sort of things that school boards  have to face  teachers have to face parents have to  face and that affect a lot of people's  lives  you know so questions of should we  actually be carrying out  for example special education  or should we just mainstream these kids  would they be better off would they  generate would it would generate more  happiness  more pleasure over pain to put kids who  have special  really you know sometimes severe special  needs into classrooms with all the other  kids  think about all the different things  that would go into this what are some of  the the pains that might occur  what are some of the negative outcomes  that could happen  yeah the children with special needs  socially as well  yeah so it can lead to fights and  bullying and  you know tension yeah distracting  like classroom environment yeah it can  make it very difficult for the kids who  are you know not brilliant but are good  to pay attention to progress to get as  much out of their education i mean we  never have enough time for teaching  anyway  so if we're going to spend half an hour  explaining to little johnny over here  why it's not okay to you know  blurt things out in class that's taking  away class time from the rest of  the students isn't it any other negative  outcomes  you can think of maybe 