rt of that  if you wanted to change that you could  make a utilitarian argument for that it  would be better for society as a whole  to have more  equality in education or you know  on the other hand and again it all  depends on how you assess the numbers  maybe it's better to have a system like  we currently do have where  you know if you want to go to a better  school move to a better school district  if you don't care about that sort of  thing if other things matter to you more  you don't you know it doesn't matter to  you what kind of school your kids are  going to  live wherever you want maybe maybe  that's the system to have from a  utilitarian perspective  let's think about how classes want to be  set up  how hard should classes be  if you make them too hard  they're less enjoyable right have any of  you ever been in classes that were too  easy for you  are those enjoyable yeah that is a  certain extent you get to screw around  but no why not  yeah you feel like you're wasting your  time  yeah um especially when you're paying  for it  too now right um how much  homework should i should i give students  i can look at that from the utilitarian  perspective what will actually produce  the greatest amount of benefit to my  students  there's you know too much that's going  to produce more pain  more negative outcomes than benefits  there's too little  students won't get that much out of it  then there's the  goldilocks region of the just right  isn't there  should i use a lot of active  learning  as they call it i'm gonna actually put  it in quotes because i'm gonna  make it versus something else  you know should i for example have like  divided you up into  pairs and you do utilitarian  calculations and then come up to the  board  each pair and explain the situation that  i gave you and then go through that  would that have been  greater benefit to you as students than  actually you know sort of more  traditional lecture format  if it would have been of greater b